@node	5.2, 5.3, 5.1, Chapter 5
@section A Register-Machine Simulator

In order to gain a good understanding of the design of register machines, we
must test the machines we design to see if they perform as expected.  One way
to test a design is to hand-simulate the operation of the controller, as in
@ref{Exercise 5.5}.  But this is extremely tedious for all but the simplest
machines.  In this section we construct a simulator for machines described in
the register-machine language.  The simulator is a Kotlin library around one
class.  Constructing a @code{Machine} with the given registers, operations,
and controller builds the model (a data structure whose parts correspond to
the parts of the machine to be simulated); writing a register's content,
running the controller to a halt, and reading a register's content simulate
the machine:

@example kotlin
Machine(⟨@var{register-names}⟩, ⟨@var{operations}⟩, ⟨@var{controller}⟩)
@end example

@noindent
constructs a model of the machine with the given registers, operations, and
controller.

@example kotlin
machine.reg(⟨@var{register-name}⟩).content = ⟨@var{value}⟩
@end example

@noindent
stores a value in a simulated register in the given machine.

@example kotlin
machine.reg(⟨@var{register-name}⟩).content
@end example

@noindent
returns the contents of a simulated register in the given machine.

@example kotlin
machine.run()
@end example

@noindent
simulates the execution of the given machine, starting from the beginning of
the controller sequence and stopping when it reaches the end of the sequence.

@noindent
As an example of how this class is used, we can build a model of the
@abbr{GCD} machine of @ref{5.1.1} as follows.  The controller is data, a
Kotlin list in the register-machine language of @ref{5.1.5}, with one
constructor call per line of the machine's diagram:

@example kotlin
public val gcdController: List<Stmt> =
    listOf(
        Label("test-b"),
        Test(OpCond("=", listOf(Source.RegSrc("b"),
            Source.ConstSrc(GValue.VLong(0L))))),
        Branch("gcd-done"),
        Assign("t",
                Source.OpSrc("rem",
                    listOf(Source.RegSrc("a"),
            Source.RegSrc("b")))),
        Assign("a", Source.RegSrc("b")),
        Assign("b", Source.RegSrc("t")),
        Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("test-b")),
        Label("gcd-done"),
    )
@end example

@example kotlin
val gcdMachine = Machine(setOf("a",
        "b",
    "t"),
    arithOperations,
    gcdController)
@end example

@noindent
The first argument to @code{Machine} is the set of register names.  The
next argument is the operations table, a map pairing each operation name with
the host function that implements the operation (that is, produces the same
output value given the same input values); @code{arithOperations} holds the
section's arithmetic, including @code{rem} and @code{=}.  The last argument
specifies the controller as a list of labels and machine instructions, as in
@ref{5.1}.  The machine's label table is built at construction by the
assembly check (@ref{5.2.2}): a duplicated label definition, an unknown
label target, or a label standing where an operation operand belongs is a
typed @code{MachineProgramError}.

To compute @abbr{GCD}s with this machine, we set the input registers, run
the machine, and examine the result when the simulation terminates:

@example kotlin
val machine = Machine(setOf("a",
        "b",
    "t"),
    arithOperations,
    gcdController)
either @{
    machine.reg("a").content = GValue.VLong(206L)
    machine.reg("b").content = GValue.VLong(40L)
    machine.run()
    machine.reg("a").content
@}
// Right(VLong(2))
@end example

@noindent
This computation will run much more slowly than a @code{gcd} procedure written
in Kotlin, because we will simulate low-level machine instructions, such as
@code{assign}, by much more complex operations.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.7}Exercise 5.7:} Use the simulator to test the
machines you designed in @ref{Exercise 5.4}.
@end quotation

