@subsection The Machine Model

The machine model is an object of class @code{Machine}.  Where the book's
model is a procedure with local state that dispatches on messages, the
techniques developed in @ref{Chapter 3}, the edition's machine is an object
whose local state is the class's fields and whose interface is its methods;
the object boundary keeps the same separation, and the one method a variant
machine calls between instructions, @code{step}, is the seam the monitoring
exercises of @ref{5.2.4} use.  This basic machine model is essentially a
container for some registers and a stack, together with an execution
mechanism that processes the controller instructions one by one.

Constructing the @code{Machine} builds the registers, keeps the operation
registry and the controller, and indexes the controller's labels with the
assembly check of @ref{5.2.2}:

@example kotlin
public class Machine(
    regs: Set<Reg>,
    public val ops: Map<String, MachineOp>,
    public val controller: List<Stmt>,
) @{
    public val registers: Map<Reg,
        Register> = regs.associateWith(::Register)

    public val stack: Stack = Stack()

    public val labels: Map<String, Int> =
        assemble(controller).fold(@{ emptyMap() @},
            @{ it.labels @})

    public var pc: Int = 0
        private set

    public var testFlag: Boolean = false
        private set

    public var instructions: Long = 0
        private set
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Registers

The book represents a register as a procedure with local state, as in
@ref{Chapter 3}; the edition's register is an object holding one machine
word.  The class @code{Register} creates a register that holds a value that
can be accessed or changed:

@example kotlin
public class Register(
    public val name: Reg,
) @{
    public var content: GValue = GValue.VUnassigned
@}
@end example

@noindent
A register holds @code{GValue.VUnassigned} until its first write; reading a
never-assigned register raises @code{UnassignedRegister}.  The name rides
along because the machine's own reports need it (@ref{Exercise 5.18} traces
every store by it).  Registers are read and written through
@code{machine.reg(name).content}; an undeclared name is a machine fault.

@subsubheading The stack

We can also represent a stack as an object with local state.  The class
@code{Stack} creates a stack whose local state consists of the items on the
stack.  A stack accepts requests to @code{push} an item onto the stack, to
@code{pop} the top item off the stack and return it, to @code{peek} at the
top item without removing it, and to @code{initialize} the stack to empty.

@example kotlin
public class Stack @{
    private val data = ArrayDeque<GValue>()

    public var pushes: Long = 0
        private set

    public var maxDepth: Long = 0
        private set

    public val depth: Int get() = data.size

    public fun push(v: GValue) @{
        data.addLast(v)
        pushes++
        if (data.size.toLong() > maxDepth) maxDepth = data.size.toLong()
    @}

    context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
    public fun pop(): GValue = data.removeLastOrNull() ?:
        r.raise(GuestError.UnassignedRegister(NO_POSITION))

    context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
    public fun peek(): GValue = data.lastOrNull() ?:
        r.raise(GuestError.UnassignedRegister(NO_POSITION))

    public fun initialize() @{
        data.clear()
        pushes = 0
        maxDepth = 0
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The book's stack signals an empty pop as a host error; this edition's stack
raises the typed @code{UnassignedRegister} fault through the run's
@code{Raise} scope.  The monitoring counters of @ref{5.2.4}---total pushes,
current depth, and high-water mark---are the same class's fields; the seam
exercises read them directly, and @code{peek} reads the top for the argument
frame without popping.

@subsubheading The basic machine

The @code{Machine} class, shown in
@ref{Figure 5.13,,@w{Figure 5.13}}, constructs
an object whose local state consists of the registers by name, the monitored
stack, the operation registry, and the controller with its label table.  The
program counter @code{pc} is an integer field: the index of the next
instruction to execute.  The test flag @code{testFlag} carries a
@code{Test} result to the next @code{Branch}.  The @code{instructions}
counter records executed instructions for the monitoring exercises of
@ref{5.2.4}.

@noindent
@strong{@anchor{Figure 5.13}Figure 5.13:} @math{\downarrow} The @code{Machine}
class, which implements the basic machine model.

@example kotlin
public class Machine(
    regs: Set<Reg>,
    public val ops: Map<String, MachineOp>,
    public val controller: List<Stmt>,
) @{
    public val registers: Map<Reg,
        Register> = regs.associateWith(::Register)

    public val stack: Stack = Stack()

    public val labels: Map<String, Int> =
        assemble(controller).fold(@{ emptyMap() @},
            @{ it.labels @})

    public var pc: Int = 0
        private set

    public var testFlag: Boolean = false
        private set

    public var instructions: Long = 0
        private set

    context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
    public fun reg(name: Reg): Register = registers[name] ?:
        r.raise(GuestError.UnassignedRegister(NO_POSITION))

    public fun halted(): Boolean = pc >= controller.size

    context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
    public fun run() @{
        while (!halted()) step()
    @}

    context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
    public fun step() @{
        if (halted()) return
        instructions++
        when (val s = controller[pc]) @{
            is Label -> pc++
            is Assign -> @{
                reg(s.reg).content = evalSource(s.src)
                pc++
            @}
            is Perform -> @{
                evalAction(s.act)
                pc++
            @}
            is Test -> @{
                testFlag = isTrue(evalOp(s.cond.name,
                    s.cond.args))
                pc++
            @}
            is Branch -> pc = if (testFlag) labelPc(s.label) else pc + 1
            is Goto ->
                pc =
                    when (val to = s.to) @{
                        is GotoTarget.Lbl -> labelPc(to.name)
                        is GotoTarget.ByReg -> regToPc(to.reg)
                    @}
            is Save -> @{
                stack.push(regContent(s.reg))
                pc++
            @}
            is Restore -> @{
                reg(s.reg).content = stack.pop()
                pc++
            @}
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The test flag is used to control branching in the simulated machine.
@code{Test} instructions set the flag to the result of the test;
@code{Branch} instructions decide whether or not to branch by examining the
flag.

The @code{pc} determines the sequencing of instructions as the machine
runs.  Where the book's @code{pc} is a register whose contents is the
not-yet-executed tail of the instruction sequence, the edition's @code{pc}
is an integer field: the index of the next instruction to execute.  In the
simulation model, the @code{step} procedure interprets the statement under
@code{pc} directly---there are no per-instruction execution procedures---and
@code{run} repeats @code{step} until @code{halted} reports that @code{pc}
has walked past the end of the controller sequence.

@noindent
As part of its operation, each @code{step} advances @code{pc} to indicate
the next instruction to be executed.  @code{Branch} and @code{goto}
instructions set @code{pc} to the destination; all other instructions simply
advance @code{pc}, making it point to the next instruction in the sequence.

Notice that running the machine is accomplished by calling @code{run}, which
repeats @code{step} to a halt.  @code{Step} is also the seam the monitoring
exercises of @ref{5.2.4} use: executing one instruction at a time is how the
breakpoint exercises park the machine.

For convenience, registers are read and written through
@code{machine.reg(name).content}, as specified at the beginning of @ref{5.2}:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
public fun Machine.reg(name: Reg): Register
@end example

@node	5.2.2, 5.2.3, 5.2.1, 5.2
@subsection The Assembler

The assembler transforms the sequence of controller expressions for a machine
into a corresponding list of machine instructions, each with its execution
procedure.  Overall, the assembler is much like the evaluators we studied in
@ref{Chapter 4}---there is an input language (in this case, the
register-machine language) and we must perform an appropriate action for each
type of expression in the language.

The register-machine controller is a typed list of @code{Stmt} constructors,
so the instruction form and its operands are already represented in fields.
Unlike the book's assembler, this edition does not compile every statement to
an execution closure.  The machine interprets the current statement directly
in @code{step}; assembly checks that labels are unique, jump destinations
exist, and labels do not appear as operation operands.

The @code{assemble} procedure scans the controller to construct the label
index and returns either a typed refusal or an @code{AssemblySummary}.  The
controller list remains the machine's execution sequence; the label index is
used by @code{Branch} and @code{Goto}.

The @code{assemble} procedure is the main entry to the assembler.  It takes
the controller list and checks it before it runs: labels resolve once,
jumps target labels, and a label never stands where an operation operand
belongs.  It answers either the first typed @code{MachineProgramError} or
an @code{AssemblySummary} with the label table and the instruction count
(the census of exercise 5.12):

@example kotlin
public fun assemble(controller: List<Stmt>): Either<MachineProgramError,
    AssemblySummary> @{
    val labels = linkedMapOf<String, Int>()
    for ((index, statement) in controller.withIndex()) @{
        if (statement is Label) @{
            if (labels.containsKey(statement.name)) @{
                return Either.Left(
                    MachineProgramError.DuplicateLabel(
                        statement.name,
                    ),
                )
            @}
            labels[statement.name] = index
        @}
    @}
    for (statement in controller) @{
        val target =
            when (statement) @{
                is Branch -> statement.label
                is Goto -> (statement.to as? GotoTarget.Lbl)?.name
                is Assign -> (statement.src as? Source.LabelSrc)?.name
                else -> null
            @}
        if (target != null &&
            !labels.containsKey(target)) @{
            return Either.Left(
                MachineProgramError.UnknownLabel(target),
            )
        @}
        val operands =
            when (statement) @{
                is Assign ->
                    (statement.src as? Source.OpSrc)?.args.orEmpty()
                is Test -> (statement.cond as? OpCond)?.args.orEmpty()
                is Perform -> (statement.act as? OpAct)?.args.orEmpty()
                else -> emptyList()
            @}
        for (argument in operands) @{
            if (argument is Source.LabelSrc) @{
                return Either.Left(
                    MachineProgramError.LabelOperand(argument.name),
                )
            @}
        @}
    @}
    return Either.Right(
        AssemblySummary(labels,
            controller.size.toLong()),
    )
@}
@end example

@noindent
Scanning the controller for labels is the first half of the check: each
@code{Label} records its index, and a repeated name is the first typed
refusal:

@example kotlin
for ((index, statement) in controller.withIndex()) @{
    if (statement is Label) @{
        if (labels.containsKey(statement.name)) @{
            return Either.Left(
                MachineProgramError.DuplicateLabel(statement.name),
            )
        @}
        labels[statement.name] = index
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The second half resolves every jump target and rejects a label wherever an
operation operand belongs---a @code{Source.LabelSrc} inside an @code{OpSrc},
@code{OpCond}, or @code{OpAct} argument is @code{LabelOperand}, the lesson
of @ref{Exercise 5.9}.  The machine keeps the checked controller as is and
the label table for its jumps; there is no separate instruction sequence
and no per-instruction execution procedure to generate.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.8}Exercise 5.8:} The following register-machine
controller is ambiguous, because the label @code{here} is defined more than
once:

@example kotlin
listOf(
    Label("start"),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("here")),
    Label("here"),
    Assign("a", Source.ConstSrc(GValue.VLong(3L))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("there")),
    Label("here"),
    Assign("a", Source.ConstSrc(GValue.VLong(4L))),
    Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("there")),
    Label("there"),
)
@end example

@noindent
What does @code{assemble} answer for this controller?  The duplicate-label
refusal is the check the book asks you to add: say which answer carries it,
and what would happen without the check (which index would the label table
keep, and what would register @code{a} hold when control reaches
@code{there})?
@end quotation

