@subsection Executing Machine Instructions

There are no execution procedures to generate: the engine interprets each
controller statement directly in @code{step}, dispatching on the sealed
@code{Stmt} family.  The work the book does once per instruction at assembly
time---resolving registers, labels, and operations---happens on every step
through the same small helpers, which is what makes this section short.  For
each type of instruction in the register-machine language, @code{step} does
the following.  Each case runs inside the run's @code{Raise<GuestError>}
scope, so a machine fault raises a typed guest error.

@subsubheading @code{Assign} instructions

An @code{Assign} evaluates its source and writes the target register:

@example kotlin
is Assign -> @{
    reg(s.reg).content = evalSource(s.src)
    pc++
@}
@end example

@noindent
The source is a register's content, a constant value, a label's index, or
an applied operation over operand sources:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
private fun evalSource(src: Source): GValue =
    when (src) @{
        is Source.RegSrc -> regContent(src.reg)
        is Source.ConstSrc -> src.v
        is Source.LabelSrc -> GValue.VInt(labelPc(src.name))
        is Source.OpSrc -> evalOp(src.name, src.args)
    @}
@end example

@noindent
Reading a register whose content is still @code{VUnassigned}---or naming a
register the machine never declared---raises @code{UnassignedRegister}.
The book's selector procedures (@code{assign-reg-name},
@code{assign-value-exp}) are not needed: the statement types already carry
their parts as fields, so a malformed instruction is unrepresentable.

@subsubheading @code{Test}, @code{branch}, and @code{goto} instructions

A @code{Test} evaluates its condition and sets the test flag; a
@code{Branch} jumps to its label when the flag is set:

@example kotlin
is Test -> @{
    testFlag =
        when (val c = s.cond) @{
            is OpCond -> isTrue(evalOp(c.name, c.args))
        @}
    pc++
@}
is Branch -> pc = if (testFlag) labelPc(s.label) else pc + 1
@end example

@noindent
A @code{Test} over a non-@code{Boolean} value is a @code{ShapeFault}:
conditions are Boolean-only, as in the guest language itself.

A @code{Goto} jumps to a label or to wherever a register says:

@example kotlin
is Goto ->
    pc =
        when (val to = s.to) @{
            is GotoTarget.Lbl -> labelPc(to.name)
            is GotoTarget.ByReg -> regToPc(to.reg)
        @}
@end example

@noindent
A register used as a jump target holds either the label's index or the
label's name; anything else is a @code{ShapeFault}:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
private fun regToPc(reg: Reg): Int =
    when (val v = regContent(reg)) @{
        is GValue.VString -> labelPc(v.value)
        is GValue.VInt -> v.value
        is GValue.VLong -> v.value.toInt()
        else -> r.raise(GuestError.ShapeFault(NO_POSITION))
    @}
@end example

@subsubheading Other instructions

@code{Save} pushes a register's content; @code{Restore} pops into a
register; @code{Perform} runs an operation for its effect; a @code{Label}
is skipped:

@example kotlin
is Save -> @{
    stack.push(regContent(s.reg))
    pc++
@}
is Restore -> @{
    reg(s.reg).content = stack.pop()
    pc++
@}
is Perform -> @{
    evalAction(s.act)
    pc++
@}
is Label -> pc++
@end example

@subsubheading Execution procedures for subexpressions

Operations run through the registry: @code{evalOp} evaluates the operand
sources and applies the named host function, and @code{evalAction} does the
same for effect.  An operation receives guest values and answers a guest
value or raises a typed guest error:

@example kotlin
public typealias MachineOp = Raise<GuestError>.(List<GValue>) -> GValue
@end example

@noindent
The trace rendering of @ref{4.4} prints one line per instruction from the
constructor fields:

@example kotlin
public fun traceLine(instruction: Stmt): String =
    when (instruction) @{
        is Label -> "$@{instruction.name@}:"
        is Assign ->
            "assign " + instruction.reg + " <- " +
                sourceText(instruction.src)
        is Test -> "test $@{condText(instruction.cond)@}"
        is Branch -> "branch $@{instruction.label@}"
        is Goto -> "goto $@{targetText(instruction.to)@}"
        is Save -> "save $@{instruction.reg@}"
        is Restore -> "restore $@{instruction.reg@}"
        is Perform -> "perform $@{actionText(instruction.act)@}"
    @}
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.9}Exercise 5.9:} An operation must operate only on
registers and constants, never on labels: @code{assemble} refuses a
@code{Source.LabelSrc} wherever an operation operand belongs, with the
