@subsection Monitoring Machine Performance

Simulation is useful not only for verifying the correctness of a proposed
machine design but also for measuring the machine's performance.  For example,
we can install in our simulation program a ``meter'' that measures the number
of stack operations used in a computation.  To do this, we modify our simulated
stack to keep track of the number of times registers are saved on the stack and
the maximum depth reached by the stack, and add an operation to the stack's
interface that prints the statistics, as shown below.  We also add an operation
to the basic machine model.  The stack and the machine already keep every
needed counter, so no new operations are installed:

@noindent
The stack already keeps the needed counters: @code{push} advances total
pushes and the high-water mark, @code{initialize} empties the stack and
zeroes them, and the machine counts executed instructions itself.  No new
operations are needed; a run's measurements are read directly---total pushes
from @code{stack.pushes}, deepest depth from @code{stack.maxDepth},
instruction count from @code{machine.instructions}---and pinned as ordinary
values.

@ref{Exercise 5.15} measures the shipped instruction counter.
@ref{Exercise 5.16} through @ref{Exercise 5.19} ask for additional tracing
and breakpoint features.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.14}Exercise 5.14:} Measure the number of pushes and
the maximum stack depth required to compute @math{{n!}} for various small values of
@math{n} using the factorial machine shown in @ref{Figure 5.11}.  From your data
determine formulas in terms of @math{n} for the total number of push operations
and the maximum stack depth used in computing @math{{n!}} for any @math{{n > 1}}. Note
that each of these is a linear function of @math{n} and is thus determined by two
constants.  In order to get the statistics, you will have to call
@code{stack.initialize()} before each run and read @code{stack.pushes} and
@code{stack.maxDepth} after it.  You may want to also modify the machine so
that it repeatedly reads a value for @math{n}, computes the factorial, and
prints the result (as we did for the @abbr{GCD} machine in @ref{Figure 5.4}),
so that you will not have to repeatedly write @code{reg("n").content} and
call @code{run}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.15}Exercise 5.15:} This edition's
@code{Machine.instructions} field already counts executed controller
instructions.  Measure the factorial machine for several values of
@math{n} and record the count after @code{machine.run()}.  Construct a fresh
@code{Machine} for each trial; its instruction counter starts at zero, and
the public API does not provide a reset message.  Explain which instruction
forms contribute to the count.
@end quotation


@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.16}Exercise 5.16:} Augment the simulator to provide
for @newterm{instruction tracing}.  That is, before each instruction is
executed, the simulator should print the text of the instruction.  Make the
machine model accept @code{trace-on} and @code{trace-off} messages to turn
